### 3.6.18 Custom Object Initialization

An object is not directly initialized via Pex. So, Pex does not create any parameterized test cases in your test class.

To get around this, your test class should contain a test method for each public method to test. Here is an example.

```csharp
namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        //...

        [TestMethod]
        public void Test_Add()
        {
            PexTest.TestMethod(typeof (ArrayListContractTest), "PUT_AddContract");
        }

        [TestMethod]
        public void Test_Remove()
        {
            PexTest.TestMethod(typeof (ArrayListContractTest), "PUT_RemoveContract");
        }

        //...

    } // class
} // namespace
```

The first parameter to `PexTest.TestMethod` is the type of the test class, and the second parameter is the fully qualified method name to be considered by Pex to create test cases.

The above Test method names correspond to the method names in the partial class definition. The Test methods are empty because they make actual calls to `PexTest.TestMethod`.

### 3.6.19 Exclude/ExcludeFromCodeCoverage attributes

You can use the Exclude and ExcludeFromCodeCoverage attributes in your test methods and other members.

- Exclude: When applied to a field or property, method, or class, Pex will exclude this member from all code paths and tests. In addition, Pex will not report any test outcomes for this member in the code coverage result.

- ExcludeFromCodeCoverage: When applied to a field or property, method, or class, Pex will exclude this member from all code paths and tests. Pex will continue to report any test outcomes for this member in the code coverage result.

For example:

```csharp
using Microsoft.Pex.Framework;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        //...

        [Exclude]
        public void Test_Dummy()
        {
            // This is a test method that just contains a method call to PexTest.TestMethod.
            // Notice: Pex will not create any test case for this method.
            PexTest.TestMethod(typeof (ArrayListContractTest), "PUT_RemoveContract");

        }

        //...

        [ExcludeFromCodeCoverage]
        public void Test_Dummy1()
        {
            // This will also be treated as Exclude.
            // The test method will not appear in the Pex generated test methods.
        }

    } // class
} // namespace
```

### 3.6.20 How Pex chooses the value for an argument

When Pex creates a test case, it chooses the value for a method argument based on several rules:

1. The argument must not be read-only.

2. We prefer scalar arguments that are of a smaller type than any other values accepted by the method. For example, if the method accepts an int, prefer a byte, then a short, etc.

3. We prefer values from the same class hierarchy. For example, if the method accepts an object, prefer a child class, then a grandchild class.

4. For comparable arguments, prefer smaller values first. For example, if T is an integer type, we prefer 0, then -1, then 1.

5. We prefer immutable instances with no invariants. Immutable instances with no invariants are generally safer to test, as the result is predictable.

6. We prefer values from instances currently in scope.

If multiple values satisfy these conditions, Pex chooses the one that is closest to the expected input. For instance, for enumeration types, we prefer a value that is "closer" to zero.

For mutable object arguments, Pex chooses an instance currently in scope if one exists. Otherwise, it creates a new instance if the class has a parameterless constructor. If neither instance nor new instance is available, it reports an error and aborts the current test case generation effort.

For scalars, Pex can directly provide values from a predefined distribution or use a seed value to--------------------
