In the `PUT_RemoveContract` function, PUT means that this method tests if the `ArrayList` behaves correctly when removing an item `x`.

An array list is generated automatically for testing with `[PexGenerateTestParameter]` attribute on `ArrayList list` and for the `target` integer with `[PexGenerateTestParameter]` attribute. For this case, the `target` is the integer number to be removed. The function calls the `RemoveContract` to execute the test. If any error occurs during the testing process, the system will automatically generate the error message.

In the `RemoveContract`, we record the initial state before removing the item and observe any changes in the state after removing the item. The result is validated to ensure the changes conform to the contract conditions.

The `Assert.IsTrue(New_arrListCount == New_arrListLastIndexOfX, $"ArrayList Count {New_arrListCount} should be same as LastIndexOf X {New_arrListLastIndexOfX}");` is a condition validated through the `Debug.Assert\(.*?\);` assertion that the ArrayList count is not same as LastIndexOf x in the new ArrayList.

This is a typical unit testing in .NET. The main steps and rules are automatically verified in the testing process.

This example shows how to perform test automation with the Parameterized Unit Testing Extension (PUT) and how to test the behavior of removing an item in an ArrayList with ContractTestCase.

Keep in mind that this test framework performs a lot of work automatically. As a result, the effectiveness of tests depends on your code coverage and the tests themselves. It doesn't guarantee that your code is bug-free but it provides a good first check, helping you to identify potential problems early on.

## Conclusion
Automated unit testing in .NET using PUT and ContractTestCase can greatly decrease the time spent on manual testing. Parameterized Testing is a technique that can significantly reduce the time and effort to design the input values for unit tests so that the same tests can be re-used. However, it must be noted that automated testing alone can never replace manual testing, particularly for more complex scenarios and edge conditions. Always have thorough manual testing to ensure comprehensive coverage. 

 


  


   

   



 




  




 






     
   
    

 



   

   
  





     
  




      
       











